heta ,z),$ where \n\n- $r\\ge 0$ and $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ are the usual plane polar coordinates, and\n\n- $z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ is the usual Cartesian $z$-coordinate.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our equation:\n\n$$\nz=3\n$$\n\nThis equation requires that $z$-coordinate of any point on our surface equals $3,$ while $r$ and $\\theta $ can vary arbitrarily in their respective domains. \n\nTherefore, our surface is the plane that is parallel to the $xy$-plane shown below.\n\n![](images/ab/ab98ba68465b36fcc3c7ac2de4f50a8f3f16f5e7e10f2c08625cbd933c59f3c4.png)" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399433 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399434 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399433 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399435 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399433 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399436 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399433 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399437 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399433 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399438 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399433 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399433 1077 "selection" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399433 1079 #edb/ref 17592186399436 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399433 1080 #uuid "f35e2e43-0eed-5ef2-9529-98c9c99a49c7" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399434 1081 "A cone" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399434 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399434 1084 #uuid "25597751-6416-5ceb-91cb-601deaf1fd9b" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399435 1081 "A half-plane that passes through the $z$-axis" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399435 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399435 1084 #uuid "eaf0226c-3780-550c-9206-c7ed7218262a" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399436 1081 "A plane that is parallel to the $xy$-plane" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399436 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399436 1084 #uuid "3b0aa564-3f7e-5327-a8c8-1edf032ad3dd" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399437 1081 "A half-cone" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399437 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399437 1084 #uuid "ec9e7d27-4886-5176-9858-61f489c8b0db" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399438 1081 "A circular cylinder" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399438 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399438 1084 #uuid "fd280fe8-8330-515c-b61b-f9b9b8476f89" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399439 1068 #edb/ref 17592186399440 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399439 1069 "q-104236" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399439 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399439 1071 "What surface is defined by the equation ${z}^{2}=4{r}^{2}$ in cylindrical polar coordinates?" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399439 1072 #uuid "f99fad58-2ad5-52cb-8779-f22e2997958e" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399439 1074 "The position of a point in cylindrical polar coordinates is written as $(r,\\theta ,z),$ where \n\n- $r\\ge 0$ and $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ are the usual plane polar coordinates, and\n\n- $z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ is the usual Cartesian $z$-coordinate.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our equation:\n\n$$\n{z}^{2}=4{r}^{2}\n$$\n\nThis equation requires that the square of the distance from any point on the surface to the $xy$-plane equals $4$ times the square of the distance from that point to the $z$-axis. In other words, the tangent of the angle between any position vector on the surface and the $z$-axis must be equal to\n\n$$\n\\frac{r}{z}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{r}^{2}}{{z}^{2}}}=\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{{r}^{2}}{4{r}^{2}}}=\\pm \\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nSince the tangent of this angle can be a positive or negative number, the $z$-coordinate can also be positive or negative.\n\nOn the other hand, $\\theta $ can vary arbitrarily in its domain. \n\nTherefore, our surface is the cone shown below.\n\n![](images/7c/7c8d0cefa5eb8da76f6cba59570670e4078c278c801aeb12344dce3183733149.png)" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399440 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399441 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399440 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399442 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399440 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399443 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399440 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399444 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399440 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399445 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399440 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399440 1077 "selection" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399440 1079 #edb/ref 17592186399441 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399440 1080 #uuid "a3e58f04-a3aa-5025-b3f4-1d84092aa5ea" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399441 1081 "A cone" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399441 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399441 1084 #uuid "dad703c3-2424-5fc7-a7e2-7cdbd0fd3e2e" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399442 1081 "A half-plane that passes through the $z$-axis" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399442 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399442 1084 #uuid "c30f7111-a28b-57da-8d7e-ebcc59c3fd70" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399443 1081 "A sphere" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399443 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399443 1084 #uuid "02bb600e-803e-556b-aa29-aeb145a011b0" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399444 1081 "A plane that is parallel to the $xy$-plane" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399444 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399444 1084 #uuid "97be7a2c-b317-5f2f-802a-fdce30804bb7" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399445 1081 "A circular cylinder" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399445 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399445 1084 #uuid "11005b22-0b1f-5136-95e7-619b8932e0bf" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399446 1068 #edb/ref 17592186399447 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399446 1069 "q-104693" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399446 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399446 1071 "Given the functions $f(x),g(x)$ and $h(x)$ below, which of them has a jump discontinuity at $x=4?$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={\\begin{aligned}5, & x<4 \\\\ {x}^{2}-10, & x\\ge 4\\end{aligned} \\\\ g(x) & ={\\begin{aligned}5-2x, & x\\le 4 \\\\ x+3, & x>4\\end{aligned} \\\\ h(x) & ={\\begin{aligned}5x, & x\\ne 4 \\\\ 5, & x=4\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399446 1072 #uuid "b7deaa4c-aeeb-5438-8da3-4314b016404f" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399446 1074 "For $y=f(x),$ let's go through our continuity checklist:\n\n- $f(4)={4}^{2}-10=6$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- Let's now check the left and right limits. We see that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {4}^{-}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to {4}^{-}}5=5\n$$\n\nwhile\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {4}^{+}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to {4}^{+}}({x}^{2}-10)={4}^{2}-10=6.\n$$\n\nSo both the left and right limits exist and are finite, but they are not equal. $\\,\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x=4.$\n\nNow, for $y=g(x),$ let's go through our continuity checklist:\n\n- $g(4)=5-2(4)=-3$ exists  $\\,\\,✓$ $\\,\\,\\,$\n\n- Let's now check the left and right limits. We see that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {4}^{-}}g(x)={lim}_{x\\to {4}^{-}}(5-2x)=5-2(4)=-3\n$$\n\nwhile\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {4}^{+}}g(x)={lim}_{x\\to {4}^{+}}(x+3)=4+3=7.\n$$\n\nSo both the left and right limits exist and are finite, but they are not equal. $\\,\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, $g(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x=4.$\n\nFinally, for $y=h(x),$ let's go through our continuity checklist:\n\n- $h(4)=5$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- Let's now check the left and right limits. We see that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {4}^{-}}h(x)={lim}_{x\\to {4}^{-}}5x=5(4)=20\n$$\n\nwhile\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {4}^{+}}h(x)={lim}_{x\\to {4}^{+}}5x=5(4)=20.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the overall limit exists and ${lim}_{x\\to 4}h(x)=20.$\n\n- Finally, we check that the value of the limit equals the value of the function:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 4}h(x)=20\\ne h(4)\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nSo the value of the limit does not equal the value of the function.\n\nTherefore, $h(x)$ has a removable discontinuity at $x=4.$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399447 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399448 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399447 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399449 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399447 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399450 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399447 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399451 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399447 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399452 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399447 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399447 1077 "selection" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399447 1079 #edb/ref 17592186399448 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399447 1080 #uuid "4fb0ec18-8ed9-5ea3-80aa-7d898a1ea97e" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399448 1081 "$f(x)$ and $g(x)$ only" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399448 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399448 1084 #uuid "70879cb5-6de6-5c72-8bca-efcd9e00d5ad" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399449 1081 "$f(x)$ only" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399449 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399449 1084 #uuid "64fa8265-bf29-50e5-a85f-52766a310a0e" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399450 1081 "$g(x)$ and $h(x)$ only" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399450 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399450 1084 #uuid "74a6e98c-2555-5075-8ac8-02954d0ca6be" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399451 1081 "$g(x)$ only" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399451 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399451 1084 #uuid "acd84fc2-8564-5e54-9ef8-6e0e0bcd95c0" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399452 1081 "$f(x)$ and $h(x)$ only" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399452 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399452 1084 #uuid "82a92379-88b6-5257-8966-edf92b316844" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399453 1068 #edb/ref 17592186399454 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399453 1069 "q-104701" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399453 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399453 1071 "Given the functions $f(x),g(x)$ and $h(x)$ below, which of them has a jump discontinuity at $x=7?$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={\\begin{aligned}2x-1, & x\\le 7 \\\\ {x}^{2}-30, & x>7,\\end{aligned} \\\\ g(x) & ={\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}, & x<7 \\\\ 5x+14, & x\\ge 7,\\end{aligned} \\\\ h(x) & ={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{x-7}, & x<7 \\\\ \\frac{1}{7}, & x\\ge 7,\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399453 1072 #uuid "24bfaf28-dbb7-577c-ab56-7ff13a09d906" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399453 1074 "For $f(x),$ let's go through our continuity checklist:\n\n- $f(7)=2(7)-1=13$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- Let's now check the left and right limits. We see that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{7}^{-}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to \\,{7}^{-}}(2x-1)=2(7)-1=13\n$$\n\nwhile\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{7}^{+}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to \\,{7}^{+}}({x}^{2}-30)={7}^{2}-30=19.\n$$\n\nSo both the left and right limits exist and are finite, but they are not equal.$\\,\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x=7.$\n\nNow, for $g(x),$ let's go through our continuity checklist:\n\n- $g(7)=5(7)+14=49$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- Let's now check the left and right limits. We see that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,{7}^{-}}g(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,{7}^{-}}{x}^{2}=49, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to \\,{7}^{+}}g(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,{7}^{+}}(5x+14)=5(7)+14=49.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the overall limit exists and ${lim}_{x\\to 7}f(x)=49.\\,\\,✓$ $\\,\\,\\,$\n\n- Finally, we check that the value of the limit equals the value of the function: \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,7}g(x)=49=g(7).\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\nTherefore, $g(x)$ is continuous at $x=7.$\n\nFor $h(x),$ let's go through our continuity checklist:\n\n- $h(7)=\\frac{1}{7}$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- Let's now check the left and right limits. We see that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{7}^{-}}h(x)={lim}_{x\\to \\,{7}^{-}}\\frac{1}{x-7}=-\\infty \n$$\n\nwhile\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{7}^{+}}h(x)={lim}_{x\\to \\,{7}^{+}}\\frac{1}{7}=\\frac{1}{7}.\n$$\n\nSince the left limit is infinite, $h(x)$ does not have a jump discontinuity at $x=7.$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399454 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399455 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399454 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399456 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399454 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399457 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399454 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399458 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399454 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399459 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399454 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399454 1077 "selection" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399454 1079 #edb/ref 17592186399456 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399454 1080 #uuid "13f403fd-c2cf-5ac8-a693-4329792d5baa" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592