es test is inconclusive.\n\nFirst, notice that since ${f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-y & 8{y}^{2}-x\\end{aligned}],$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}={x}^{2}-y,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=8{y}^{2}-x.\n$$\n\nThe first derivative exists for any $(x,y)$ in the domain of the function, so we only need to find the stationary points. Setting ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T},$ we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-y=0 \\\\ 8{y}^{2}-x=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-y=0\\,⟹\\,y={x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nThen, substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8{({x}^{2})}^{2}-x & =0 \\\\ 8{x}^{4}-x & =0 \\\\ x(8{x}^{3}-1) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBack-substituting these values into $y={x}^{2},$ we obtain\n\n$$\nx=0\\,\\,\\,\\,⟹\\,y={0}^{2}=0,\\,\\,x=\\frac{1}{2}\\,⟹\\,y={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}=\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nHence, $(0,0)$ and $(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{4})$ are stationary points of $f(x,y).$\n\nTo classify these stationary points, we use the second partial derivative test.\n\nFirst, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-y)=2x\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(8{y}^{2}-x)=-1 \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-y)=-1\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(8{y}^{2}-x)=16y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2x & -1 \\\\ -1 & 16y\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the second derivative at each of the stationary points and use it to categorize them:\n\n- Evaluating the second derivative at $(0,0),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(0,0)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant $H(0,0),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(0,0)=det({f}^{″}(0,0))={0}^{2}-(-1)^{2}=-1<0,\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(0,0)$ is a saddle point.\n\n- Evaluating the second derivative at $(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{4}),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{4})=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant $H(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{4}),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{4})=det({f}^{″}(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{4}))=1\\cdot 4-(-1)^{2}=3>0\n$$\n\nFinally, since $H(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{4})>0$ and $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{4})={h}_{11}=1>0.$ Therefore, we have a local minimum at $(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{4}).$\n\nIn conclusion, $f(x,y)$ has a saddle point at $(0,0)$ and a local minimum at $(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{4}).$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399475 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399476 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399475 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399477 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399475 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399478 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399475 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399479 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399475 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399480 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399475 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399475 1077 "selection" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399475 1079 #edb/ref 17592186399476 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399475 1080 #uuid "905988c5-8a51-5ced-aa99-8546923bfeea" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399476 1081 "$f(x,y)$ has a saddle point at $(0,0)$ and a local minimum at $(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{4})$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399476 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399476 1084 #uuid "984bdcd8-65fe-53ce-9365-592fb416acc7" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399477 1081 "$f(x,y)$ has a saddle point at $(0,0)$ and a local maximum