he second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nFirst, we compute the first derivative of $f(x,y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f′(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-{y}^{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-{y}^{2})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{x} & \\operatorname{ln}⁡x-2y\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe first derivative exists for any $(x,y)$ in the domain of the function, so we only need to find the stationary points. Setting $f′(x,y)={0}^{T},$ we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{x}=0 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡x-2y=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{y}{x}=0\\,⟹\\,y=0.\n$$\n\nThen, substituting this into the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-2(0)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x=0\\,⟹\\,x=1.\n$$\n\nHence, $(1,0)$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y).$\n\nTo classify this stationary point, we use the second partial derivative test.\n\nFirst, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{y}{x})=-\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}}\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-2y)=\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{y}{x})=\\frac{1}{x}\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-2y)=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative  is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}} & \\frac{1}{x} \\\\ \\frac{1}{x} & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(1,0),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(1,0)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant $H(1,0),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(1,0)=det({f}^{″}(1,0))=0\\cdot (-2)-{1}^{2}=-1<0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the second partial derivative test, we have that $f(x,y)$ has a saddle point at $(1,0).$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399482 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399483 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399482 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399484 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399482 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399485 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399482 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399486 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399482 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399487 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399482 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399482 1077 "selection" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399482 1079 #edb/ref 17592186399484 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399482 1080 #uuid "7119470b-d6b1-5d18-94e0-b83f9d1bc35d" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399483 1081 "$f(x,y)$ has a local minimum at $(e,0)$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399483 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399483 1084 #uuid "3afd00a5-3aad-54a4-9a70-7c2101ecdf05" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399484 1081 "$f(x,y)$ has a saddle point at $(1,0)$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399484 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399484 1084 #uuid "31d284b3-9581-5047-bd3c-06f5026f21ae" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399485 1081 "$f(x,y)$ has a local maximum at $(1,0)$" 1319